{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>And he called it Novach</b> – The letter heh is not written with a mappik. I saw in the work of Rabbi Moshe ha-Darshan: since this name did not endure for it, therefore it is weak (rapheh), for the implication of his midrash is as though it reads \"lo\" (no). And I am astonished — what would he interpret from two similar verses: \"And he said to her, Boaz\" (Ruth 2:14), \"to build her a house\" (Zechariah 5:11) — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 32:42). Yet the Rav is a sealed storehouse for Torah, for halakhot and for aggadot, and this one escaped him, which they said in Midrash Ruth (Ruth Rabbah 5:5): \"\"And I am not like one of your handmaidens\" (Ruth 2:13) — he said to her: Heaven forbid, you are not of the maidservants but of the matriarchs. And similarly: \"And Novach went and captured Kenath and called it Novach after his name\" — this teaches that that name did not endure for it. And similarly: \"And he said to me, to build her a house in the land of Shinar\" — this teaches that falsehood has no remedy. Thus far in the Aggadah. And likewise in the Gemara of tractate Sanhedrin (24a) they said: this is hypocrisy and haughtiness — when they went down to Babylonia, there is no rival to Babylonia, and the proud one seeks a still higher refuge in Elam; precisely therefore it is written \"to build her a house\" — learn from it. And this is a midrash derived from the absence of this mappik, that the house in the land of Shinar did not endure."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Rashi as deriving the midrash from the defective spelling (without mappik); he then cites the Rav (Rashi) as a comprehensive authority whose collection of sources nevertheless missed this parallel aggadah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:10:12.477956730Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}